        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c4632a58-3488-56fb-9b03-cfed335e494c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Istanbul, Turkey, Ayakapı, Sultanahmet Meydanı\n- Buildings: Gül Mosque, Hippodrome of Constantinople\n- Concepts: Eastern Orthodox church, mosque, Byzantine buildings, Ottoman conversion\n\nFacts:\n- Gül Mosque is in Istanbul, Turkey.\n- Gül Mosque was formerly an Eastern Orthodox church and was converted into a mosque by the Ottomans.\n- Ayakapı contains the Gül Mosque.\n- Gül Mosque is one of the most important surviving Byzantine buildings on Istanbul's historical peninsula.\n- The Hippodrome of Constantinople is today a square in Istanbul named Sultanahmet Meydanı.\n- Only a few fragments of the original Hippodrome of Constantinople survive.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "06fe31f8-5498-5093-a618-cffc81ddd291",
        "text": "Page: Gül Mosque. Sentence: Gül Mosque (Turkish: \"Gül Camii\" , meaning: \"The Mosque of the Rose\" in English) is a former Eastern Orthodox church in Istanbul, Turkey, converted into a mosque by the Ottomans.\nPage: Hippodrome of Constantinople. Sentence: Today it is a square named \"Sultanahmet Meydanı\" (\"Sultan Ahmet Square\") in the Turkish city of Istanbul, with a few fragments of the original structure surviving.\nPage: Ayakapı. Sentence: In Ayakapı lies one of the most important surviving Byzantine buildings of the historical peninsula, the Gül Mosque.",
